course: reflection
lesson: 11
title: Cringe that lasts: getting over embarrassing moments
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some embarrassing moments make you cringe for weeks: the memory jumps out when you're cleaning your teeth, and your whole body squirms as if it were happening again.
explain: Callum got knocked out of the spelling bee on the word "because", the audience went "Oooh", and on the way home he kept thinking, "BECAUSE. A baby word. I can spell 'necessary' but not 'because'. I'm such a numpty"; that night he wanted the ground to swallow him.
explain: Under pressure, easy things go wrong because nerves block memory for a moment, so it said nothing about how clever he was; when Dad told his own spelling-bee disaster, they laughed together, and a month later Callum was saying "because" in a silly voice himself.
explain: Turning a cringe into a shared joke takes the sting out, and nicknames usually die down fastest when you shrug instead of exploding; what doesn't help is practising the word a hundred times at midnight or hiding under a hat.
explain: And if someone shares an unkind photo or comment, the cringe is theirs to own: you can ask for it to be deleted and say it hurt.
fact: Under pressure, nerves can block memory for a moment, so even easy things go wrong; "my mind went blank" describes this.
fact: Turning an embarrassing moment into a shared family joke can take the sting out of it.
fact: Hair grows back, usually about one centimetre a month; the first days after a big change in looks feel the worst, then people get used to it.
fact: Unkind comments about looks are often someone grabbing a cheap laugh, and they say more about the speaker than about you.
fact: Nicknames often fade when the person stops reacting; getting angry usually makes teasers say them more.
fact: A pet name is a loving nickname from family; asking a parent privately not to use it in public is fair.
fact: Everyone loses control sometimes, especially when overtired; a meltdown is losing control of your feelings, and a simple sorry afterwards usually fixes it.
fact: Asking someone something, like to dance, takes courage; being turned down hurts but is normal, and an unkind "ew" says more about the person showing off to friends.
fact: Feeling embarrassed by your family is common at this age and does not mean you love them less.
fact: Head lice are very common and can happen to anyone; lice do not care whether hair is clean or dirty, so having them is not about being dirty.
fact: A bad photo is one strange moment from one angle, not what you really look like.
fact: Taking or posting photos of people to laugh at them is unkind, and it is okay to ask for them to be deleted.
fact: Everyone picks their nose or dribbles in their sleep sometimes; these are normal body things, not proof that someone is gross.
exercise: Callum went out of the spelling bee on "because", though he can spell "necessary". Why? | Nerves can block memory for a moment, even for easy words | He secretly can't spell at all and was just lucky in every test before | Easy words are always harder than long words | The judges gave him a trick word on purpose
exercise: What helped Callum get over the spelling bee? | Dad telling his own disaster and laughing about it together | Practising "because" a hundred times at midnight until he was exhausted | Never entering any competition ever again | Telling everyone the judges cheated
exercise: Molly's hair was cut very short by mistake and a boy made a mean joke about her looks. What is true? | Hair grows back, and the first days after a change feel worst | Her hair will look like this for the rest of her life | She should stay off school until it grows back | The boy is right, so she should wear a hat forever
exercise: What helped Molly stop wearing her hat? | A friend saying it looked cool, and a new hairclip | Wearing the hat in every lesson for months until her hair grew | Cutting the boy's hair to get even | Refusing to look in any mirror at all
exercise: Tyler's mum shouted "Tyler-Wyler!" at football and now his team calls him that. What made them stop? | Laughing along so they got bored | Getting angry every single time they said it | Asking the coach to ban the name at training | Quitting the team for a whole season
exercise: What else did Tyler do that was fair? | Asked Mum privately not to use the pet name in public | Shouted at Mum in front of the whole team so she'd learn | Refused to speak to Mum for a month | Told everyone it was his sister's nickname
exercise: Jade had a screaming tantrum in a shop because she was overtired, and her best friend's mum saw. What fixed it? | Saying sorry to the mum next time she saw her | Worrying about it for weeks without saying anything | Avoiding her friend's house forever | Pretending it was her little sister
exercise: Sam asked a girl to dance at the disco and she said "Ew, no" while her friends laughed. What did Sam realise later? | Asking took courage, and the "ew" said more about her showing off | He must be ugly, so he should never ask anyone to dance again, ever | Asking someone to dance is always a stupid idea | Everyone at the disco is still laughing at him
exercise: Ava asked Dad to park round the corner because his old car roars like a tractor. Then she felt bad. What is true? | Being embarrassed by family is common and doesn't mean less love | It means deep down she doesn't really love her dad as much as she says | Only spoilt children feel embarrassed by family | Her dad should buy a new car to fix it
exercise: Mo got head lice and kids called him "Nitty Mo". What is true about lice? | They're common and don't care if hair is clean | Only dirty children who never wash ever get head lice | Head lice mean a family doesn't wash | Lice only live on people who never brush
exercise: What made the "Nitty Mo" name stop? | The teacher saying lots of people get lice | Mo shaving all of his hair off completely bald | Mo believing that he really was a dirty kid | Everyone else catching lice the next week too
exercise: Zara's friend posted a weird-looking photo of her captioned "LOOK AT HER". What helped? | Asking the friend to delete it and telling her it hurt | Taking a hundred selfies to check what her face really looks like | Posting a worse photo of the friend back | Deleting all her own photos from her phone
exercise: Leo was caught picking his nose in class and became "Bogey Boy" for a week. What made it fade? | Shrugging it off with "yeah, and?" | Getting angry each time someone said it | Telling the teacher every five minutes | Hiding in the library at every break
exercise: Someone took a photo of Lily asleep and drooling on the bus. What is true? | Photos to laugh at people are rude; she can ask to delete it | She should never fall asleep anywhere again | Drooling in your sleep means something is wrong | It's fine to take photos of anyone who is asleep in a public place
test: Callum's mate from Edinburgh: "Ye're no a numpty, yer heid just went blank." ("Numpty" is a gentle word for a silly person; "heid" means head.) Is the mate right? | Yes; nerves can make your mind go blank on easy things | No; going blank proves you can't spell | No; only silly people's minds go blank | Yes, but it means he'll go blank every single time from now on
test: Molly says, "I'm not going to school till it grows back. That'll take a YEAR." What's the truth? | The first days feel worst; people get used to it | She's right; nobody at school will forget it for a whole year | Hair never grows back after a short cut | She should wear a wig every day until then
test: In a cartoon, a boy screams "STOP CALLING ME THAT!" every time, and the nickname vanishes. What usually happens in real life? | Angry reactions often make teasers say it more | Screaming always makes a nickname vanish at once | Nicknames never ever fade, whatever you do about them | Teasers only ever stop if you cry in front of them
test: Jade's friend's mum, Kiwi: "Sweet as, love, everyone has a meltdown when they're knackered." ("Sweet as" means it's fine; "knackered" means very tired.) What's true? | Everyone loses it when tired; a sorry fixes most of it | Only babies have meltdowns, never nine-year-olds | Mums never forgive a tantrum they've seen | Being tired makes tantrums impossible
test: Sam's mate: "Bro, you asked. Nobody else even tried. That took guts." ("Took guts" means took courage.) Why is the mate right? | Asking takes courage, and being turned down is normal | Being turned down proves Sam is brave and handsome | Nobody should ever ask anyone to dance | It only took guts because the girl was mean and everyone knew it
test: Ava's dad calls his roaring old banger "The Beast". ("Banger" means old car.) Why did laughing about it with him help? | It turned the cringe into a shared joke, not a shame | It made the car quieter, so nobody could hear it any more | It meant she could stop loving her dad and feel nothing | It made the other kids stop looking at the car forever
test: What would happen if Mo kept believing "I'm dirty" after the lice were gone? | He'd keep feeling ashamed for something that isn't true | He'd catch lice again because of the belief | It would make him cleaner than other kids | Nothing; beliefs never affect feelings
test: A kid says, "lice only go on dirty kids, everyone knows that." Is he right? | No; lice don't care whether hair is clean or dirty | Yes; washing every day keeps lice away for good | Yes; lice only live on kids who never shower | No, lice only go on kids with long hair
test: Zara stares at the posted photo: "Is that what I REALLY look like?" What's the truth? | A bad photo is one odd angle and moment, not her real face | Yes; cameras always show what people really look like | Yes; that's why she should take lots of selfies to check | No, because cameras make everyone look worse
test: Lily's American cousin says, "dude, you were dead to the world, someone got a pic lol." ("Dead to the world" means very deeply asleep.) What should Lily do? | Tell Mum and ask for the photo to be deleted | Hide down on the back seat every day from now on | Never sleep anywhere outside her own bed again | Take photos of everyone else asleep to be even
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the failure of a competent speller upon an easy word in public competition? | Nervousness may temporarily obstruct the recall of known material | Such failure establishes conclusively an inability to spell | Easy words are inherently more difficult than long words | Such failure arises solely from deliberate malpractice by the judges
exam: Identify the course of action most likely to bring about the cessation of a nickname employed by teammates. | Good-humoured indifference in place of angry reaction | Vehement objection upon every occasion of its use | Permanent withdrawal from all team activities | The adoption of an even less dignified nickname
exam: It is given that a pupil has been found to have head lice. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Head lice are common and bear no relation to the cleanliness of the hair | Head lice are confined to persons of unhygienic habits | Head lice constitute evidence of parental neglect | Head lice are acquired solely by persons with long hair
exam: Ascertain which of the following is consistent with the facts concerning a child's embarrassment at the conduct or possessions of his family. | Such embarrassment is common at this age and does not diminish affection | Such embarrassment demonstrates an absence of love for the family | Such embarrassment is confined to children of a spoilt disposition | Such embarrassment may be remedied only by the purchase of new possessions
